INT. REGIONAL BAKING CHAMPIONSHIP, COMMUNITY HALL - AFTERNOON

Bunting. Folding tables. A smell of sugar and mild panic. Contestants stand at oven stations in matching aprons.

Five of them cluster near the judges' table, where a lemon tart sits under a glass dome.

BERNADETTE "BERNIE" OKONKWO-PRYCE (60s, apron with a hand-stitched rolling pin) wipes flour from her glasses.

CORNELIUS "CORKY" ABERNATHY-LIU (30s, hair net, drawn-on mustache) sulks beside a deflated soufflé.

GWENDOLYN MUKHERJEE-FALLOWS (40s, crisp white chef's jacket) arranges a perfect row of macarons.

ORVILLE "OLLIE" TEMPLETON-ADAIR (70s, suspenders) hums off-key over a tray of shortbread.

And the judge, MADAME FRANÇOISE OLUWASEUN-LEBLANC (50s, silk scarf, expression of permanent disappointment), lowers herself into the central chair.

FRANÇOISE
Contestants. Time.

BERNIE
Time!

CORKY
Time?

GWENDOLYN
Time.

OLLIE
(humming)
Time, time, time.

FRANÇOISE
Mr. Abernathy-Liu, your soufflé has fallen.

CORKY
It's resting.

FRANÇOISE
It is flat.

CORKY
It's modernist.

BERNIE
It's a pancake, dear.

CORKY
It's an homage to the pancake.

GWENDOLYN
Mine rose perfectly.

BERNIE
Yours is a macaron, Gwen. It's required to.

GWENDOLYN
Don't be catty.

OLLIE
I'm not catty. I'm shortbread.

FRANÇOISE
Mr. Templeton-Adair, you are not allowed to hum.

OLLIE
I'm not humming.

FRANÇOISE
You are.

OLLIE
It's the oven.

The oven timer BEEPS. Everyone jumps. Gwendolyn's elbow knocks her tray. Macarons scatter across the floor like pastel dice. Corky dives, Bernie dives, Ollie dives. The three of them collide, flour clouds going up, a tangle of aprons.

FRANÇOISE
(standing)
Stop! Stop at once!

Bernie surfaces with a macaron in her teeth. Corky has a mouthful of shortbread. Ollie is under the table. Gwendolyn stands frozen, hands at her cheeks.

GWENDOLYN
(whispering)
Eight hours.

FRANÇOISE
Mrs. Mukherjee-Fallows.

GWENDOLYN
Eight hours I stood at that oven.

BERNIE
(spitting out the macaron)
It's not bad, actually.

CORKY
(muffled)
Quite good.

OLLIE
(from under the table)
Can someone pass a plate?

Françoise sits slowly, looks at the wreckage, and for the first time lifts a hand to her mouth to hide a smile.

FRANÇOISE
I have never seen such a thing.

BERNIE
Does that mean we won?

FRANÇOISE
It means I'm hungry.
